# p5/contract/namespace -- the ON-DISK NAMESPACE P5 MAY OWN.
#
# This file is a RULE, not an inventory. `contract/paths` is the inventory.
# The installer refuses any destination that does not match a line here
# (exit 4), so a later unit's packaging cannot silently claim a path outside
# the product's namespace. That refusal is the enforcement point named in
# ROADMAP U26 ("install/uninstall is the only correct enforcement point").
#
# FORMAT: role|pattern
#   role      client | server | both   (which box the pattern applies to)
#   pattern   POSIX `case` glob, absolute, PRODUCTION path (no test-root prefix).
#             In `case`, `*` matches `/` too, so `/etc/p5/*` covers nesting.
#
# WHY THESE PATTERNS AND NOT OTHERS -- every one is derived, none is a taste call:
#
#  1. OpenWrt/GL.iNet stock filesystem layout decides the ROOTS: executables in
#     /usr/sbin, product data + libraries in /usr/lib, configuration in /etc,
#     procd services in /etc/init.d, netifd hooks in /etc/hotplug.d/iface,
#     volatile runtime state in /var/run (tmpfs).
#
#  2. The `p5` INFIX is forced by the standalone-product requirement
#     (p5-execution-handover.md:8 "cleanly UNINSTALL the old package, then
#     install P5 fresh"). E7 must be able to remove exactly P5 and exactly the
#     old stack, with no path claimed by both -- otherwise "remove precisely
#     those and nothing else" is undecidable, and a rollback to the frozen
#     P1-P3 baseline cannot be reasoned about. The old stack occupies
#     /etc/bond, /usr/sbin/{bond-*,bondctl,engarde-*}, /etc/init.d/{bond-*,
#     engarde-*,cake-autorate,sqm}, /etc/hotplug.d/iface/{97-bond,
#     98-wg-autorate,99-tether-autorate}, /etc/wg-*, /root/cake-autorate,
#     /var/run/bond (enumerated in contract/foreign). Disjointness from that
#     set is the requirement; the `p5` infix is the cheapest way to satisfy it.
#     CONSEQUENCE, stated so it is not discovered later: the datapath binary
#     CANNOT install as /usr/sbin/bond-agg, because that path is on the old
#     stack's removal list. It installs under a p5-* name.
#
#  3. The hotplug priority is a WILDCARD `[0-9][0-9]`, not a number. Choosing a
#     number is choosing an ordering against netifd's other hooks; that is a
#     derivation the owning unit (E5/E6) must make and justify. E0 refuses to
#     invent one. The same wildcard rule covers the procd rc.d flags below.
#
#  4. /etc/rc.d IS in the namespace, because P5 CREATES paths there. On OpenWrt
#     `/etc/init.d/<svc> enable` writes /etc/rc.d/S<pri><svc> and K<pri>
#     symlinks, and E5 ships /etc/init.d/p5-datapath and /etc/init.d/p5-server
#     (contract/paths). The deployed stack already treats that flag as
#     lifecycle STATE in writing (p2-engarde/bondctl:15 "State owners:
#     engagement = rc.d flag", p1-autorate/autoratectl:11,
#     98-wg-autorate:9, 99-tether-autorate:49). Leaving the pattern out did not
#     stop P5 from creating those symlinks; it only stopped anything from
#     knowing about them, so E7 would have left stale flags pointing at deleted
#     init scripts. They are created AFTER the installer exits, so they can
#     never reach installed.files -- contract/paths carries them with
#     state=enable and E7 removes them with `disable`, the mechanism that made
#     them. The name is anchored to `p5-` so the pattern cannot reach a flag
#     belonging to any other service on the box.
#
# NOT owned, deliberately:
#   /var/log/*   -- OpenWrt logging is syslog via `logger`; a P5 log file is a
#                   decision no one has made. If a unit needs one it adds the
#                   pattern here with its reason.
#   /root/*      -- the old stack scattered binaries and helpers there. P5 does
#                   not, so nothing of P5's is ever in an operator's home dir.
#   /etc/config/*-- UCI. Whether P5's facts are UCI or plain files is E5/E6's
#                   call, so /etc/config/p5 is listed in contract/paths as
#                   `reserved` and is admitted by the pattern below only if
#                   that unit keeps it. If E5 chooses plain files, delete the
#                   pattern rather than leaving an unused claim standing.

both|/usr/sbin/p5
both|/usr/sbin/p5-*
both|/usr/lib/p5
both|/usr/lib/p5/*
both|/etc/p5
both|/etc/p5/*
both|/etc/config/p5
both|/etc/init.d/p5-*
both|/etc/rc.d/[SK][0-9][0-9]p5-*
both|/var/run/p5
both|/var/run/p5/*
client|/etc/hotplug.d/iface/[0-9][0-9]-p5
